\details{
For each tissue and trait:
\enumerate{
\item \strong{Donors.} The tissue's samples with a value of the trait and of
every covariate. A covariate column with the name of the tested trait is
left out (in the paper, sex and age were covariates except when tested).
The trait is skipped with fewer than \code{control$min_samples} donors or no
variation (\code{"trait_constant_or_n"}), or, for a binary trait, fewer than
\code{min_group} donors in a group (\code{"trait_rare"}).
\item \strong{Covariates.} Numeric columns are used as they are. A categorical
column (factor or character) becomes indicators of its levels, after the
levels with fewer than \code{min_level_count} donors are merged into the most
common level (the reference). Columns that are constant on the donors
are dropped. The columns are standardised over the donors and an
intercept is added.
\item \strong{Coding.} A binary trait (two distinct values) is coded so that
the contrast is the lower value to the higher (0 to 1; for a two-level
factor, the first level to the second); a continuous trait so that the
contrast is the donors' mean to one standard deviation above it.
\item \strong{Tests.} Each feature is fitted and tested as in \code{\link[=bohr_fit]{bohr_fit()}} and
\code{\link[=bohr_test]{bohr_test()}}, with the fitting rules of \code{control} (SI Section 2): mu
with the F-corrected likelihood-ratio test, p0 and p1 with the
Firth-penalised likelihood-ratio test. For a binary trait, a feature
needs at least \code{min_group} donors in each group among its samples, and
mu also among its intermediate samples.
\item \strong{Units and mirror pairs.} One unit per feature and component (mu,
p0 when zeros remain, p1 when ones remain) with a finite P value. With
\code{promoters$gene}, exact mirror pairs are found in each tissue
(\code{\link[=mirror_pairs]{mirror_pairs()}}, among the features with data in that tissue) and
de-duplicated as in \code{\link[=bohr_cis]{bohr_cis()}}: for a \code{boundary_exact} pair the p1
units of both members are dropped (each boundary event is tested as p0
at the promoter that goes silent); for an \code{interior_exact} pair the mu
unit of the partner is dropped.
\item \strong{Eligibility and q values.} A unit is \code{eligible} (enters the
Benjamini-Hochberg correction) when it is not a mirror duplicate, a mu
unit has at least \code{min_n_mid} intermediate samples, a p0 or p1 unit has
a boundary share within \code{boundary_share}, a p0 or p1 unit's boundary
part is not completely separated by the covariates
(\code{exclude_separated}), and the tissue and trait have at least
\code{min_donors} donors. q values are computed within each tissue, trait and
component; \code{significant} is q at most \code{fdr}.
\item \strong{Labels of exact-pair boundary events.} With \code{absolute}, each
significant p0 unit of a \code{boundary_exact} pair is labelled by absolute
activity (SI Section 2): the absolute activity of each promoter of the
pair is regressed (least squares) on the coded trait and the covariates,
over the donors; a promoter changes when \eqn{|z| \ge} \code{abs_z}. When
only the other promoter changes, the event is reported as p1 at that
promoter (its own fit, the same test) with the q value of the p0 test
(\code{bh_family = "p0"}, \code{abs_relabel = TRUE}), and the p0 row becomes the
duplicate. Without \code{absolute}, every such event stays p0 at the silent
promoter (the rule "v3m"), and \code{settings$mirror_labels} says so.
}
